WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.410
JENNY BROWN: Hi, I'm Jenny.

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.790
And today I'd like
to talk to you

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:06.250
about a question that comes up
from enterprises quite a bit.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.360
How do you choose when and
where to use service management

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.240
and when and where to
use API management?

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.120
While it may feel
like there's overlap,

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.870
I'm here to highlight
the differences that

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.690
make it easy to pick the right
technology for your use cases.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.080
First, we'll do a quick overview
on service management and API

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:31.892
management.

00:00:31.892 --> 00:00:34.350
And then we're going to jump
into the modernization journey

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.360
as well as some use cases.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.170
We'll summarize
everything at the end.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:40.510
But for now, let's
dive right in.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.720
First, let's cover the basics.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.050
What are service management
and API management?

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.250
Service management is usually
done with a service mesh.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.070
It manages the communications
between services,

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.860
and it's focused on handling
overall service to service

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.230
policies or telemetry.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.830
API management is usually
done with an API platform.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.709
It's all about managing
the life cycle of APIs

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:07.650
and is geared towards API
publishing, consumption,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.980
governance, and receiving
analytics of usage of APIs

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.840
by consumers.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.590
Those sound pretty different,
so what's the confusion about?

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:17.970
Here's the thing.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.850
Both service management
and API management

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.400
are an essential part of an
enterprise's modernization

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.880
journey.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.070
So the real question is not
which one to choose, rather,

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.840
when and where.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.460
You might choose
one over the other

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.400
depending on where you are in
your modernization journey.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.880
Or you might choose API
management for one use case

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.760
and service management for a
different part of your business

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.810
with a different use case.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.260
If you're involved in a
modernization journey,

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.830
then you most likely have one
or both of the following goals--

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.830
to modernize applications
with cloud-native technologies

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:56.580
to deliver software faster
or transform businesses

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.760
by giving developers, partners,
and customers unique ways

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.890
to interact with your products.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.780
To accomplish both
of these goals,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:08.039
you'll need both the service
management and API management.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:10.050
Let's walk through
six scenarios where

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.500
you can decide where and when
to choose service management

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.110
and/or API management.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.260
First up.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.280
Imagine your organization is
building an internal platform

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.340
with requirements around
standardized security policies

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.840
and controls.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.270
Here, a service management
layer will give you

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.420
all the capabilities you need to
secure and control the traffic

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.430
within that platform.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.880
Next, your SRE team
wants to monitor

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.630
the health and performance
of the services

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.490
within that platform.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.650
In this scenario,
service management

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.060
can provide your team
with the metrics, logs,

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:47.490
and tracing so they can
set SLOs and get alerts.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:50.010
And what about when a
network operator on your team

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.830
needs to configure networking
components so that services can

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.520
communicate across clusters
in hybrid and/or multi cloud

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.170
situations?

00:02:58.170 --> 00:02:59.940
Service management
provides you a way

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.520
to standardize networking
across those environments

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.570
and components.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.380
And now imagine that this
platform is owned by a business

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.270
unit within an enterprise.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.370
They might want to set
up a shared service model

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.220
where other business units
can discover and reuse

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.250
some of those standardized
services as APIs.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.190
In this case, the goal is
to provide easy visibility

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.370
for other teams, which are
outside the domain of trust,

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.700
into what APIs are available
and how to use them.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.100
An API management
platform is a great way

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.690
to govern this process.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.390
It would provide the
mechanism to expose these APIs

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.540
to other teams, provide
access to these services,

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.480
and the tools to measure
API usage through analytics.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.050
Here, this organization
is now at a point

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.970
where they want to bring
third-party partners

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.130
and developers into the mix.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.290
This could be to adhere
to industry practices,

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.930
such as open banking, or
to expand your business

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.880
model to new channels.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:57.240
Similar to our last use case,
an API management platform

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.670
will let you securely
expose those services

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.430
outside of your organization
and give you the tools

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.190
and provide easy
onboarding and access.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.470
Now that you've given all
these stakeholders access

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.320
to those APIs, you want to
see how they're being used

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.720
and potentially even
explore some monetization

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.160
opportunities.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.589
This is another scenario
where an API management

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.480
platform is a great fit.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.690
You can get visibility into
API consumption and growth,

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.840
determine which
APIs are valuable,

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.160
and put monetization
models around APIs

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.920
that you want to charge
third parties for.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.620
As you can see, there are
many distinct use cases

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.770
for service management
and API management.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:37.960
Depending on your needs, you
may start with one or the other.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.020
You may use them for
different initiatives.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.590
Or you may even use both service
management and API management

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.200
together to perform different
functions for a platform

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:47.910
that you're building.

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.920
To summarize, if
you're primarily

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.260
looking for a technology
for service-to-service

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.500
communication that maintains
the quality of service

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.290
in a system composed of
distributed services,

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:01.010
then service management
is the best fit.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.680
And if you're looking to drive
consumption of those services

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.190
as APIs by different
business units or partners

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:10.190
and build new business
channels, then API management

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:11.690
is the best fit.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:15.590
In our experience, both service
management and API management

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:18.290
are crucial in any
organization's application

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.030
modernization journey.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:22.940
Here at Google, we provide
service management capabilities

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:26.300
through Anthos Service Mesh,
which is a part of Anthos,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.320
and API management
capabilities through Apigee.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.750
Check out these links
below for more information,

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:34.250
and let us know
what else you'd like

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.730
to learn about Google Cloud.

